    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay explores Japanese graphic novels that feature male homosexuality, a genre known as yaoi. While this genre has mostly been explored from the perspective of feminist studies, the author offers an alternative critical framework that merges the perspectives of art history and media studies by reading yaoi next to nanshoku, a form of Japanese visual art popular in the 17th and 18th centuries. The differences in how both genres configure masculinity,\r\nthe author shows, can be traced (in some surprising ways) to Western influences on a longer history of male homosexual practices in Japanese society.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
